    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Junior Caminero** serving as our highest-upside play at a 67.23, despite a daunting matchup against Gausman. While Gausman\u2019s xERA of 3.72 suggests a challenge, **Junior Caminero** benefits significantly from hitting second and a park factor that favors his profile, keeping him ahead of **Yordan Alvarez** (64.21). **Yordan Alvarez** remains a must-start staple given his elite 0.429 xwOBA, though the matchup against Kirby dampens his ceiling slightly. The most surprising value today comes from **Casey Schmitt** (63.20); projected to bat third, he draws a favorable matchup against Roki Sasaki\u2019s 5.30 xERA. The massive +19% boost from the opposing SP skill and a projected top-of-the-order spot make **Casey Schmitt** an essential plug-and-play against a pitcher who has struggled with command early in the season.\n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, particularly with the bottom of our order. **Hyeseong Kim** (41.20) sits dangerously close to our \"below floor\" threshold; while he has a platoon advantage against Trevor McDonald, the high risk of a strikeout-heavy outing against a pitcher with a 2.60 xERA\u2014combined with the possibility of him hitting eighth\u2014makes him a volatile asset. **Kazuma Okamoto** (49.62) and **Luke Raley** (52.39) provide stability as marginal starts, but neither offers the explosive upside we typically crave. Unfortunately, our bench is largely compromised; with **Trea Turner**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, and **Kyle Schwarber** sidelined by a team off-day, and **Christian Yelich** remaining on the IL, we lack the depth to pivot away from our lower-scoring starters. We are essentially running with our available personnel, keeping a close watch on **Hyeseong Kim**\u2019s official lineup position, as a drop in the order could plummet his efficiency score into the sub-40 range.",
